          "level": 1,
          "content": "5.3 Some Other Processes Involving Chemical Changes"
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "bbox": [
            0.272,
            0.167,
            0.446,
            0.189
          ],
          "level": 2,
          "content": "5.3.1 Rusting"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.254,
            0.195,
            0.657,
            0.326
          ],
          "content": "In the rusting of iron, which you studied in the chapter ‘The World of Metals and Non-metals’, a new brown-coloured substance called rust is formed. Thus, rusting is also a chemical change because it involves the formation of a new substance, iron oxide (Fig. 5.4)."
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "bbox": [
            0.684,
            0.172,
            0.877,
            0.308
          ],
          "content": "",
          "captions": [],
          "footnotes": [
            {
              "bbox": [
                0.712,
                0.307,
                0.838,
                0.338
              ],
              "content": "Fig. 5.4: Rusted iron nails"
            }
          ],
          "image_source": "images/page_5_image_5.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "bbox": [
            0.062,
            0.341,
            0.385,
            0.511
          ],
          "content": "",
          "captions": [
            {
              "bbox": [
                0.086,
                0.517,
                0.373,
                0.534
              ],
              "content": "Fig. 5.5: Burning magnesium ribbon"
            }
          ],
          "footnotes": [],
          "image_source": "images/page_5_image_7.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "bbox": [
            0.395,
            0.337,
            0.626,
            0.359
          ],
          "level": 2,
          "content": "5.3.2 Combustion"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.394,
            0.366,
            0.884,
            0.441
          ],
          "content": "Let us recall the burning of a magnesium ribbon (Fig. 5.5) learnt in the chapter ‘The World of Metals and Non-metals’. Can you **predict** if this is a physical or a chemical change?"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.394,
            0.442,
            0.883,
            0.553
          ],
          "content": "When the magnesium ribbon is burnt, a new substance, magnesium oxide, is formed. So, the burning of magnesium ribbon also involves a chemical change. We observed that heat and light are also produced along with the formation of a new substance in this reaction."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.256,
            0.554,
            0.831,
            0.613
          ],
          "content": "The burning of magnesium ribbon can be represented as— Magnesium + Oxygen Magnesium oxide + Heat + Light (Ribbon) (Air) (White powder)"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.254,
            0.615,
            0.883,
            0.707
          ],
          "content": "A chemical reaction in which a substance reacts with oxygen and produces heat and/or light is called **combustion**. Substances that undergo combustion reactions are called **combustible substances**. For example, wood, paper, cotton, kerosene, etc., are combustible substances."
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "bbox": [
            0.063,
            0.709,
            0.375,
            0.877
          ],
          "content": "",
          "captions": [],
          "footnotes": [],
          "image_source": "images/page_5_image_14.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.075,
            0.882,
            0.335,
            0.915
          ],
          "content": "Fig. 5.6: Candle (a) burning (b) covered with a glass tumbler"
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "bbox": [
            0.014,
            0.921,
            0.27,
            0.984
          ],
          "content": "",
          "captions": [],
          "footnotes": [],
          "image_source": "images/page_5_image_16.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.39,
            0.717,
            0.882,
            0.754
          ],
          "content": "Let us find out whether the presence of oxygen is essential for combustion."
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "bbox": [
            0.407,
            0.766,
            0.712,
            0.786
          ],
          "level": 2,
          "content": "Activity 5.5: Let us investigate"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.403,
            0.798,
            0.882,
            0.837
          ],
          "content": "Place two identical candles on two separate petri dishes and light them."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.403,
            0.841,
            0.882,
            0.879
          ],
          "content": "Cover one of these with a glass tumbler, as shown in Fig. 5.6."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.403,
            0.883,
            0.882,
            0.92
          ],
          "content": "What happens to the candle flames in the two cases?"
        },
